---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Principle from "../components/Principle.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

import ScaleCostChart from "../components/visuals/ScaleCostChart.astro";
import CitationCard from "../components/visuals/CitationCard.astro";
import OutputDiff from "../components/visuals/OutputDiff.astro";
import AIBadge from "../components/visuals/AIBadge.astro";
import PipelineIO from "../components/visuals/PipelineIO.astro";
import IterationLoop from "../components/visuals/IterationLoop.astro";
import UpstreamComms from "../components/visuals/UpstreamComms.astro";
import RealDataGrid from "../components/visuals/RealDataGrid.astro";
import BenchmarkComparison from "../components/visuals/BenchmarkComparison.astro";
import ErrorMessages from "../components/visuals/ErrorMessages.astro";
import FeatureChecklist from "../components/visuals/FeatureChecklist.astro";
import VersionPin from "../components/visuals/VersionPin.astro";
import GovernanceChecklist from "../components/visuals/GovernanceChecklist.astro";
import HotSwapDiagram from "../components/visuals/HotSwapDiagram.astro";
import LicenseCheck from "../components/visuals/LicenseCheck.astro";
import OpenSourceFlywheel from "../components/visuals/OpenSourceFlywheel.astro";
import BugReportDecisionTree from "../components/visuals/BugReportDecisionTree.astro";
---

<Layout title="rewrites.bio — A manifesto for bioinformatics">
  <Hero />

  <main class="manifesto">
    <div class="preamble" id="introduction">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="preamble-layout">
          <div class="preamble-text reveal-stagger">
            <p class="reveal">
              Most bioinformatics tools were written for a world that no longer exists. They were
              built by brilliant scientists solving hard problems, and they work. But they were built
              when datasets were smaller, compute was slower, and a single lab could run everything
              on a desktop. Today, a typical RNA-seq study processes thousands of samples. The
              computational cost of slow, memory-hungry tools has compounded from a nuisance into a
              real scientific and environmental problem.
            </p>
            <p class="reveal">
              The scale problem is concrete. A pipeline like nf-core/rnaseq is run by thousands of
              researchers. If one step wastes ten minutes of CPU per sample and the pipeline runs on
              a million samples a year, that is nearly two decades of wasted compute, every year.
              The tools are correct, and that correctness is hard-won. But correctness and efficiency
              are not in conflict. We can have both.
            </p>
            <p class="reveal">
              Wasted CPU-hours have a physical cost: kilowatt-hours, kilograms of CO2. A widely-used
              pipeline running more efficiently is not just faster science, it is more responsible
              science. Rewrites of key bioinformatics tools that run 10-50x faster can save hundreds
              of tonnes of CO2-equivalent per year across the pipelines that use them. Efficiency is
              no longer a nice-to-have.
            </p>
            <p class="reveal">
              What has changed is the cost of solving this. AI coding assistants have shifted the
              equation. A domain expert who understands a tool's inputs, outputs, and edge cases can
              now produce a working reimplementation in days rather than years. The slow part was
              never understanding what the tool does; it was writing performant code to do it.
              That barrier has largely gone. Fast code is now cheap. Scientific insight, validation,
              and trust are not.
            </p>
            <blockquote class="preamble-callout reveal">
              A wave of AI-assisted rewrites is coming.<br />
              The question is not <strong>whether</strong> it will happen,<br />
              but whether it will happen <strong>well</strong>.
            </blockquote>
            <p class="preamble-closing reveal">These are the principles we follow.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="preamble-visual reveal">
            <ScaleCostChart />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Section
      id="philosophy"
      number="I"
      title="Philosophy"
      intro="Before writing any code, three commitments shape everything else. These are not procedural rules. They are a statement of values."
    >
      <Principle id="credit-authors" number="1.1" title="Credit the original authors">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Behind every bioinformatics tool is years of work: algorithm design, edge-case hunting,
            user feedback, and ongoing maintenance, often by people who were underpaid or
            unpaid for it. A rewrite stands entirely on that foundation. Without the original tool's
            existence, test cases, and accumulated community knowledge, a rewrite cannot even be
            built correctly, let alone validated.
          </p>
          <p>
            Credit belongs everywhere: the README, the docs, any paper or preprint, and in
            downstream reports that use results from the rewrite. A dedicated credits page with
            full citations, DOIs, author lists, and version numbers is the minimum. When users
            cite your rewrite, they should know to also cite the original.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is how the scientific community tracks provenance and assigns credit to work that
            enables everything downstream. Obscuring the lineage of a tool undermines the incentive
            structures that motivated the original work.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CitationCard slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="emulate-exactly" number="1.2" title="Emulate exactly">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The goal is not to build a better version of the tool. It is to build a faster version
            that produces the same results. A rewrite that changes outputs, even in ways that look
            like improvements, is a different tool. It cannot inherit the original's validation
            history. It cannot be quietly substituted into existing pipelines without forcing
            users to re-validate their analyses.
          </p>
          <p>
            Emulate exactly means bit-identical or numerically-identical outputs wherever possible.
            For deterministic tools, that means byte-for-byte matching files. For tools with
            floating-point arithmetic, it means results within acceptable numerical precision,
            where "acceptable" is defined by domain scientists, not implementation convenience.
            Header formats, column ordering, file naming, and summary statistics all count.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you find what looks like a bug in the original tool, do not fix it silently.
            Document the discrepancy, report it upstream (see 4.5), and let the maintainers
            decide. Your rewrite's job is to replicate the behaviour users already depend on,
            including quirks they have built downstream analyses around.
          </p>
        </div>
        <OutputDiff slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="transparent-ai" number="1.3" title="Be transparent about AI">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            When an AI coding agent writes the implementation, that is a material fact about the
            software. It affects how the code should be trusted, reviewed, and audited. It does
            not mean the software is untrustworthy; correctness is determined by output comparison,
            not by who or what wrote the code. But users and contributors deserve to know how it
            was built.
          </p>
          <p>
            Document clearly in the README and any accompanying paper: which AI tools were used,
            what role they played, and how correctness was verified. "Validated by output
            comparison against mytool v1.0 on a suite of real sequencing datasets" is far more
            useful than leaving provenance implicit. Do not obscure AI involvement to make the
            project seem more traditionally engineered than it is.
          </p>
          <p>
            Output comparison catches what you tested. It does not catch edge cases you have not
            encountered yet. Being transparent also means committing to ongoing validation as new
            datasets and use cases emerge, and being honest about where coverage is thin.
          </p>
        </div>
        <AIBadge slot="visual" />
      </Principle>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="planning"
      number="II"
      title="Planning"
      intro="The decisions made before you write the first function determine most of the outcome. Scope, validation strategy, and architecture all matter more than the choice of language."
    >
      <Principle id="think-big" number="2.1" title="Think big">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The biggest performance wins rarely come from writing faster implementations of
            existing tools. They come from rethinking the architecture. Modern bioinformatics
            pipelines chain many discrete tools, each reading from disk, doing work, and writing
            back to disk. Every one of those I/O operations is an opportunity to waste time.
            A straight port to a faster language captures only a fraction of what is available.
          </p>
          <p>
            Think about what a single integrated process could do without materialising intermediate
            files. Trimming, alignment, and quantification can share data in memory rather than
            writing gigabytes of temporary BAM files between steps. Eliminating redundant I/O is
            often worth more than any algorithmic improvement. Ask: what preprocessing does this
            tool assume has already happened? Can that be folded in? What immediately consumes
            this tool's output? Can that be folded in too?
          </p>
          <p>
            This is not a licence to build an unvalidatable monolith. It is permission to think
            beyond one-to-one replacement and ask what a pipeline designed from scratch, with what
            we now know about where the bottlenecks are, would actually look like.
          </p>
        </div>
        <PipelineIO slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="work-small" number="2.2" title="Work small">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Thinking big about architecture does not mean building big in one go. The implementation
            strategy should be the opposite: small, tight iteration loops, each step validated
            against the original before moving on. Start with the simplest function that produces
            testable output. Validate it. Then extend.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you implement several components before validating any of them, a discrepancy in
            the final output could come from anywhere. You have created a debugging problem that
            requires mentally decomposing what you just built. Work small, and when something is
            wrong, you already know where to look.
          </p>
          <p>
            AI coding agents are good at generating plausible implementations quickly. They are
            unreliable for correctness, especially in domain-specific numerical behaviour. The
            right response is not to distrust them but to validate continuously. An agent told
            "this step's output doesn't match on paired-end data with adapter sequences" can
            iterate productively. An agent asked to implement an entire pipeline in one prompt
            is much harder to correct.
          </p>
        </div>
        <IterationLoop slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="engage-upstream" number="2.3" title="Engage upstream">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The maintainers of the tool you are rewriting know its intended behaviour, its
            undocumented edge cases, and the design decisions that never made it into any paper.
            Engaging early is not just courtesy, it is useful. They may know about a tricky
            normalisation step described only in a footnote. They may have an upcoming refactor
            that would make your approach obsolete. They may want to collaborate.
          </p>
          <p>
            Open an issue or send an email before you start. Something like: "We're planning an
            AI-assisted performance rewrite. We'd like to validate against your test suite and
            would welcome guidance on the correctness criteria that matter most." Most open-source
            maintainers are not threatened by rewrites that credit their work. They are threatened
            by ones that replace them silently.
          </p>
          <p>
            At a minimum, give maintainers a heads-up before your public release. Being surprised
            to learn a popular rewrite of your tool exists, and having heard nothing about it,
            damages trust. The bioinformatics community is small. Burn relationships here and
            you feel it everywhere.
          </p>
        </div>
        <UpstreamComms slot="visual" />
      </Principle>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="building"
      number="III"
      title="Building"
      intro="Good intentions don't ship correct software. The building phase is where principles meet reality: data that behaves unexpectedly, performance that doesn't match theory, users who need the tool to work before they will trust it."
    >
      <Principle id="test-real-data" number="3.1" title="Test relentlessly with real data">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Synthetic data is useful for quick iteration. It is not sufficient for validating a
            bioinformatics rewrite. Real sequencing data has properties synthetic generators do
            not replicate: instrument-specific error patterns, real adapter contamination, quality
            score profiles that differ between chemistry versions, reads spanning difficult genomic
            regions, and samples with genuine biological variation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Test on multiple organisms, platforms, experimental designs, and library prep protocols.
            Test on old data too. Instruments have changed, and a tool that handles modern short
            reads correctly may fail on data from earlier chemistries that are still common in
            public archives. Test edge cases: empty files, single-read files, files where all
            reads fail quality filters, very large files that stress memory handling.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every test case that passes is a commitment. Maintain those cases in the repository.
            When a user reports a failure on a dataset type you haven't tested, add it to the
            suite. A well-maintained rewrite should eventually have broader test coverage than
            the original, because it is forced to be explicit about what it has verified.
          </p>
        </div>
        <RealDataGrid slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="benchmark" number="3.2" title="Benchmark rigorously">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Performance claims require evidence. "Our rewrite is 10x faster" is a marketing
            statement unless it comes with a reproducible benchmark: the hardware, the dataset,
            the exact command lines for both tools, and an output comparison confirming the results
            match. Without those details, the number cannot be verified by anyone else.
          </p>
          <p>
            Commit reference inputs and expected outputs to the repository alongside the benchmark
            scripts. Anyone who clones the repo should be able to reproduce your numbers. Benchmark
            on hardware representative of what your users have. A result on a 96-core server is
            not the same as a result on the 8-core nodes that most academic clusters provide.
            Measure wall-clock time and peak memory separately. Both matter.
          </p>
          <p>
            Benchmark honestly. If your rewrite is faster on some data types but not others, say
            so. If the original is still better in some cases, document them. A rewrite that
            claims uniform superiority and then fails for certain users will lose trust fast.
            Numbers without context mislead. Numbers without reproducibility are anecdotes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <BenchmarkComparison slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="build-for-users" number="3.3" title="Build for users">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            A tool that is 20x faster but crashes with an unhelpful error message will not get
            adopted. Users trust tools that behave predictably, communicate clearly when something
            goes wrong, and do not require reading source code to use. Performance is necessary
            but not sufficient.
          </p>
          <p>
            Error messages should say what went wrong, where, and what the user can do about it.
            "Segmentation fault" tells nobody anything useful. "Input index not found for
            sample.bam, run samtools index first, or pass --no-index to proceed without one"
            gives them a path forward in under a minute. The difference determines whether
            they try again or abandon the tool.
          </p>
          <p>
            Defaults should be sensible. The command run without reading any documentation should
            produce a useful result in the common case. Document not just what each flag does but
            when you would want to use it. Think about the user integrating your tool into a
            pipeline they did not write; they need to understand failure modes, not just the
            happy path. A fast tool that frustrates users will not displace a slow one they trust.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ErrorMessages slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="build-what-you-need" number="3.4" title="Build only what you need">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Feature completeness is not the goal. Output correctness on the features that matter
            is. Most bioinformatics tools have accumulated a long tail of flags and edge cases
            over years of user requests. Not all of those are used in the pipeline you are
            targeting. Reimplementing everything at once greatly increases the validation surface
            and the chance of shipping something broken in a less-tested path.
          </p>
          <p>
            Identify exactly which capabilities your target pipeline uses. Reimplement those, and
            reimplement them exactly. For features you have not implemented, fail with a clear
            message explaining what is not supported and pointing to the original tool for those
            cases. Do not silently ignore unsupported flags or produce wrong output for partially
            implemented features.
          </p>
          <p>
            A focused rewrite that does four things correctly is more valuable, and more adoptable,
            than one that claims to do fifteen and does twelve of them right. Scope can expand as
            the project matures and the validation suite grows. It cannot easily contract once
            users depend on it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <FeatureChecklist slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="pin-versions" number="3.5" title="Pin versions and document">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Every claim your rewrite makes about output equivalence is implicitly a claim about a
            specific version of the original tool. "Compatible with mytool" is meaningless.
            "Produces bit-identical output to mytool v1.0, validated on the dataset described in
            benchmarks/" is a testable assertion. The difference matters when a user hits an
            unexpected discrepancy six months after your release.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pin the exact version of every original tool you have validated against. Document not
            just the version number but the command line used for comparison, the dataset, the
            comparison methodology, and the date. This lives in the repository, not in a blog post
            or your memory, so contributors and users can find it without asking.
          </p>
          <p>
            When the original tool releases a new version, you need a process for deciding whether
            to update your validation target. Major version bumps often change output formats.
            Patch releases often fix bugs that may or may not affect your outputs. Document your
            policy and communicate it to users.
          </p>
        </div>
        <VersionPin slot="visual" />
      </Principle>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="stewardship"
      number="IV"
      title="Stewardship"
      intro="A rewrite is not a one-time act. Once other researchers build pipelines on your tool, you have made a commitment. Stewardship means honouring it: maintaining correctness as upstream tools evolve, governing transparently, and giving back to the ecosystem that made the rewrite possible."
    >
      <Principle id="maintain" number="4.1" title="Maintain and govern">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The hard part of releasing a bioinformatics tool is not the initial implementation.
            It is the years of maintenance that follow. Users will find edge cases you didn't test.
            The original tool will update and require re-validation. New sequencing chemistries
            will produce data your test suite doesn't cover. PRs will need reviewing. Someone needs
            to be responsible for all of this, and that needs to be visible before you ship.
          </p>
          <p>
            Put governance in place before release: a CONTRIBUTING.md, a CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, a
            defined release process with versioned tags and changelogs, and a commitment to
            acknowledge issues within a reasonable time. These are signals to potential contributors
            and users that the project takes itself seriously.
          </p>
          <p>
            Think about succession. Who takes over if you move on? Is the project structured so
            others could realistically maintain it without you? An unmaintained rewrite sitting in
            a broken state is worse than no rewrite. It gives users false confidence, blocks better
            alternatives, and poisons the well for future rewrite efforts in the same space.
          </p>
        </div>
        <GovernanceChecklist slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="preserve-compatibility" number="4.2" title="Preserve compatibility">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The strongest argument for adopting a rewrite is zero migration cost. If your rewrite
            produces identical output files, same names, same formats, same column headers, same
            summary statistics, then swapping it into an existing pipeline is a one-line config
            change. Every MultiQC module that parses the original's output will parse yours.
            Every downstream script that reads the original's results will read yours.
          </p>
          <p>
            This reversibility is what makes adoption risk-free, and risk-free adoption is what
            makes widespread adoption possible. Researchers running production pipelines cannot
            re-validate their entire analysis history every time they update a tool. But they
            will try a rewrite they can test on one run, confirm matches, and roll back if needed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compatibility extends to the CLI. If the original takes <code>--threads N</code>,
            yours should too. If it takes input as a positional argument, do not require a flag.
            Users have wrapper scripts, Nextflow modules, Snakemake rules, and documented
            procedures tied to specific interfaces. Breaking any of these, even in small ways,
            imposes real costs on real people.
          </p>
        </div>
        <HotSwapDiagram slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="license" number="4.3" title="Respect upstream licenses">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Open-source licenses are legal agreements. Before writing a line of code, read the
            license of the original tool and every library or algorithm it depends on. Permissive
            licenses (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD) let you release your rewrite under any license you
            choose. Copyleft licenses (GPL and variants) require compatible licensing on any
            software derived from them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whether a reimplementation constitutes a "derived work" legally depends on
            jurisdiction and the specific facts, and this is not legal advice. What we can say
            is: erring on the side of caution and choosing a license at least as permissive as
            the most restrictive original is both legally safer and better for the community.
            When in doubt, consult a lawyer or choose a more restrictive license.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check every license before you start, not after. Retrofitting licensing constraints
            onto a project that already has contributors, pipeline integrations, and public
            visibility is painful. License archaeology is far easier before you ship.
          </p>
        </div>
        <LicenseCheck slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="open-source" number="4.4" title="Release as open source">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The tools you are rewriting were built by the open-source scientific community,
            validated against publicly funded datasets, and distributed freely so science could
            move faster. A rewrite that takes all of that as its foundation and releases as
            closed source is extracting value from a commons without contributing back. It also
            makes the rewrite unavailable to the labs that need it most and cannot afford
            commercial licensing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Open source is also practically better for adoption. Researchers need to inspect
            source code to understand what a tool does to their data. They need to run it on
            HPC clusters where commercial software is often unavailable. They need to be able
            to fork it if the project is abandoned. A permissive license removes barriers at
            every stage.
          </p>
          <p>
            Choose MIT, Apache-2.0, or BSD unless the original tool's licensing requires
            otherwise. Copyleft licenses like GPL, while open source, create friction for
            pipelines mixing tools with different licenses. Lowering adoption barriers is the
            point.
          </p>
        </div>
        <OpenSourceFlywheel slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="contribute-upstream" number="4.5" title="Contribute upstream responsibly">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Building a rewrite puts you in an unusual position: you have studied the original
            tool closely, tested it on diverse data, and found its edge cases. If you find a
            genuine bug you can reproduce in the original tool with a minimal, clean test case,
            report it. That is a real contribution to the scientific community.
          </p>
          <p>
            But be careful before filing bug reports. AI coding agents are fluent and confident,
            and they are wrong in subtle ways that are easy to miss. An agent may convince you
            the original tool is handling a particular input incorrectly when what is actually
            happening is that your reimplementation is wrong, or the input is malformed, or you
            are misreading the spec. Before reporting upstream, verify manually with the original
            tool on clean, well-understood data, not with an AI-generated test case.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not automate bug reports. Open-source maintainers are often volunteers. A
            firehose of machine-generated issues, even polite ones, burns out people whose
            continued work benefits everyone. The bar for filing upstream should be: you are
            confident this is a real bug, you can reproduce it cleanly, and you have considered
            whether you can also contribute a fix.
          </p>
        </div>
        <BugReportDecisionTree slot="visual" />
      </Principle>
    </Section>

    <div class="closing">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="closing-line"></div>
        <p class="closing-text">These principles are a starting point, not a checklist.</p>
        <p class="closing-body">
          Every rewrite is different. Some principles will be easier to honour than others in
          your context. What matters is that the intent behind each one shapes the decisions you
          make when the easy path and the right path diverge. The wave is coming. Let's make
          sure it lifts all boats.
        </p>
        <p class="closing-attribution">
          Discuss and contribute at <a href="https://github.com/seqeralabs/rewrites.bio">github.com/seqeralabs/rewrites.bio</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .manifesto {
    padding-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  /* ── Preamble ── */
  .preamble {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    scroll-margin-top: 72px;
  }

  .preamble-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .preamble-visual {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(52px + var(--space-lg));
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .preamble-layout {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .preamble-visual {
      position: static;
    }
  }

  .preamble p {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .preamble-callout {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--rust-400);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--rust-600);
    padding-left: var(--space-lg);
    margin: var(--space-xl) 0;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .preamble-callout strong {
    color: var(--rust-300);
  }

  .preamble-closing {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.4rem);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  /* ── Closing ── */
  .closing {
    padding: var(--space-2xl) 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .closing-line {
    width: 48px;
    height: 2px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--rust-600), var(--rust-400));
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-xl);
  }

  .closing-text {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 1.6rem);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .closing-body {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    max-width: 560px;
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-lg);
  }

  .closing-attribution {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
  }

  .closing-attribution a {
    color: var(--rust-400);
  }
</style>
